{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "si-05-linked-multi-base-exponent-relations",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Linked Multi-Base Exponent Relations",
    "description": "Turn exponential links into ordinary equations between exponents, then solve the much simpler system underneath.",
    "teachingApproach": "Separate two layers every time: first convert bases and obtain relations among exponents; only then solve the linear or cyclic relation. Use checks in the original powers so learners see why a correct algebraic solution also satisfies the exponential statement.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 32,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 9, Surds and Indices. Approved scope: Teach how products such as 8^x·2^y become one equation like 3x+y=9, and how a second exponential condition supplies a second linear equation. Extend to equal-power chains such as 2^x=4^y=8^z, cyclic relations x=y^a, y=z^b, z=x^c, and telescoping chains such as 4^a=5, 5^b=6, 6^c=7, 7^d=8. Include the chapter's reciprocal-exponent relation pattern 2^x=3^y=6^(−z), and linked equations formed by converting 2/4/8 or 3/9/27 to common bases. Learners should separate the base work from the linear-equation work and check the final exponents in the original relations."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Some exponent questions look as if several different bases are fighting each other.\n\nUsually they are not. 8 is 2^3, 9 is 3^2, and a chain like 4^a=5, 5^b=6 can telescope. We will translate the powers into simpler exponent relations first.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Suppose 8^x×2^y=512.\n\nEverything is a power of 2.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "8^x×2^y=(2^3)^x×2^y=2^(3x+y)\n512=2^9\n\nSo the exponential statement becomes the linear relation 3x+y=9.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
