    "teach_2": {
      "id": "teach_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A second exponential condition can give a second linear equation.\n\n8^x×2^y=2^9 gives 3x+y=9.\n3^(3x+2y)=3^12 gives 3x+2y=12.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_2_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_2_step": {
      "id": "teach_2_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Subtract the first from the second:\ny=3.\nThen 3x+3=9, so x=2.\n\nThe difficult-looking powers reduced to a two-line system.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_2"
        }
      ]
    },
